Meaning they are trained to look for specific shapes.

124
00:08:17,420 --> 00:08:17,700
Right.

125
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:31,200
If your car's computer relies on recognizing the 3D geometry of a child, and let's say the child is wearing a bizarre Halloween costume, or is partially obscured by a mailbox.

126
00:08:31,840 --> 00:08:36,500
The object recognition model can just fail because the shape doesn't match its training data.

127
00:08:36,720 --> 00:08:37,200
Exactly.

128
00:08:37,860 --> 00:08:41,380
Yarncrawler uses a trajectory-based ontology instead.

129
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:46,360
What's fascinating here is that objects are not the starting point.

130
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:50,160
They emerge as persistent patterns in these fields over time.

131
00:08:50,860 --> 00:08:53,320
So a child isn't a pre-programmed shape.

132
00:08:53,740 --> 00:09:03,340
A child is a localized bundle of biological density moving with a specific vector and generating a specific thermodynamic entropy signature.

133
00:09:03,700 --> 00:09:04,260
Precisely.

134
00:09:04,580 --> 00:09:15,980
And the reason this is ultimately safer and far more robust is because structuring the world as RSVP fields allows the system to apply the unyielding laws of physics to the incoming data.

135
00:09:15,980 --> 00:09:19,580
Because physics equations are literally field equations.

136
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:19,920
Yes.

